\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

This paper formalizes recursive generation depth estimation as ordinal classification under controlled full-replacement chains.
The perplexity tail feature family dominates depth discrimination across all nine cells, with $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}4$ for all three families (confirmed by 5-class extension, 71--80\%; Appendix~\ref{app:depth4_ext}); cross-model transfer reaches 66.4\% (d1--d3: 55.4\%), cross-domain arXiv 74.1\%, while binary detectors and membership inference methods achieve only 30--35\%.

\paragraph{Accumulation settings.}
Our benchmark uses full-replacement chains; in practice, model training involves accumulation, mixing human data with synthetic outputs at unknown proportions \cite{gerstgrasser2024_accumulate}.
Under accumulation, depth signal should attenuate because each generation retains human-originated distributional properties, slowing tail erosion.
The 66.4\% cross-model and 59.1\% cross-strategy transfer should be interpreted as ceiling estimates for the mixed-data regime.
Extending the framework to accumulation, where depth becomes a continuous mixing parameter, is a priority for future work.

\paragraph{Scale and deployment implications.}
The depth signal attenuates with model scale: at 14B, d0/d1 separability drops sharply ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$), though d1--d3 discrimination persists above chance.
This suggests that depth estimation complements rather than replaces binary detection for practical data auditing: binary detectors handle the human-vs-machine boundary where depth estimation is redundant ($>$97\% d0 recall), while depth estimation provides the finer ordinal resolution that binary methods cannot.
A practical deployment path proceeds from controlled settings, where model and strategy are known, to partially known settings via strategy-identification routing, to fully unknown settings requiring strategy-invariant features (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).

\paragraph{Open challenges.}
Cross-strategy transfer (d1--d3: 45.9\%) remains the primary bottleneck, driven by structurally distinct feature-space trajectories across decoding strategies.
Scale-dependent signal attenuation, depth-quality entanglement, and extension to accumulation settings are open challenges.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.9--85.9\% four-class; d1--d3: 58.9--81.5\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares ordinal depth estimation methods across all 9 atlas cells.
RF, XGBoost, and LightGBM on the same 19 features converge within 0.4\,pp per cell, confirming a feature-determined ceiling; on Pythia nucleus, CORAL \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match RF within 0.6\,pp ($p>0.05$).

\paragraph{Binary detectors and membership inference.}
Four binary detection/membership inference methods, calibrated for ordinal depth, all fall 43--48\,pp below RF (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).
Min-K\% Prob \cite{shi2024_minkprob} (33.2\%) outperforms DetectGPT \cite{mitchell2023_detectgpt} (30.7\%), but CORAL ordinal calibration does not close the gap (d1--d3: 40.4\% vs.\ RF 71.6\%), confirming the bottleneck is input signal, not calibration (Appendix~\ref{app:detector_baselines}).
Adding Min-K\% as a 20th RF feature yields only +0.48\,pp (78.4\%$\to$78.9\%); the 19 statistical features already capture the dominant ordinal signal.

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2, 36.2\%; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 30.4\%) also fail (43\,pp below the best RF cell; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}); depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Ordinal depth estimation: method comparison (mean across 9 atlas cells; DetectGPT: 8 of 9, Pythia nucleus excluded due to scoring instability). Chance = 25\% (4-class) / 33.3\% (d1--d3). Min-K\%: $K{=}20$, CORAL calibration. DetectGPT: gpt2-large scoring, t5-large perturbation ($n{=}5$). 5-fold CV; SD $\leq$ 0.9\,pp for feature-based methods.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Method & 4-Cl Acc (\%) & d1--d3 BalAcc (\%) \\
\midrule
RF 19-feature (ours) & \textbf{78.4} & \textbf{71.6} \\
\midrule
Zero-shot LLM (best) & 36.2 & --- \\
Min-K\% Prob (CORAL) & 33.2 & 40.4 \\
DetectGPT (perturbation) & 30.7 & 24.3 \\
Random & 25.0 & 33.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Convergence confirms the signal resides in the features: three classifiers match within 0.8\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}), perplexity features alone recover 66.0\%, and seed replication across three families shows $\Delta{\leq}3.2$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_replication}).
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified (recall ${>}$99\%); errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread to d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.9\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%). Ordinal quality: MAE\,=\,0.39, QWK\,=\,0.81 (QWK$_{\text{d1--d3}}$\,=\,0.53); 94.8\% of errors at $|\Delta| \leq 1$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% four-class (d1--d3: 55.4\%; $2.7\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the four-class headline, and d2 recall drops to 26.8\% in the worst case (Pythia holdout).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy} reports leave-one-strategy-out transfer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Cross-strategy transfer yields 59.1\% four-class (d1--d3: 45.9\%, chance = 33\%), 7\,pp below cross-model transfer.
This gap is itself an empirical finding: different decoding strategies induce structurally distinct degradation trajectories in feature space, not merely noisier versions of the same trajectory.
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ inverts the d1/d2 boundary under temperature (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5), because temperature scaling accelerates convergence (\S\ref{sec:method:theory}).
A two-stage pipeline (strategy identification + depth classification) recovers 11.5\,pp, with a 31.5\,pp d1--d3 gap between correct and wrong routing confirming feature-space misalignment (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).

\paragraph{Prompted generation.}
Replicating the GPT-2~XL nucleus pipeline with topic-seeded generation yields 69.5\% (95\% CI $[68.9, 70.2]$, $-$13.8\,pp vs.\ BOS-seeded, $2.8\times$ chance), with monotonic AUC decay and identical top features (Appendix~\ref{app:prompted_gen}): prompt conditioning attenuates but does not eliminate the depth signal.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

Depth signal persists at larger scales: 7--8B models achieve 60--70\% and 14B (Qwen2.5-14B) reaches 54.1\% (d1--d3: 57.4\%, chance = 33\%), though confounded by five factors (quantization, sample size, single strategy, vocabulary mismatch, self-reference scoring; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_confounds}).
Cross-model chains (84.75\%) and cross-domain arXiv (74.1\%) confirm robustness at the primary 1--1.5B scale (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).

\section{LoRA Fine-Tuning Protocol}
\label{app:lora_protocol}

Table~\ref{tab:lora_protocol} lists the full LoRA fine-tuning configuration used for recursive chain construction (\S\ref{sec:method:benchmark}).
At each depth $k$, a fresh LoRA adapter is initialized on the base model and trained on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$; adapters are not inherited across depths.
The HuggingFace Trainer default linear decay schedule is used with no warmup.
Target modules are model-specific: \texttt{query\_key\_value} (Pythia), \texttt{att\_proj} (OLMo), and \texttt{c\_attn} (GPT-2~XL).
Cross-scale experiments (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale}) use QLoRA (4-bit NF4) with $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{LoRA fine-tuning hyperparameters for the primary 1--1.5B benchmark. Identical across all three model families and all depth levels.}
\label{tab:lora_protocol}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
LoRA rank $r$ & 16 \\
LoRA alpha $\alpha$ & 32 \\
LoRA dropout & 0.05 \\
Bias & none \\
Epochs per depth & 3 \\
Learning rate & $2 \times 10^{-4}$ \\
LR schedule & linear decay (no warmup) \\
Batch size (effective) & 16 ($8 \times 2$ grad.\ accum.) \\
Max sequence length & 512 tokens \\
Precision & bfloat16 \\
Adapter init & from scratch (base model) \\
Seed & 42 (primary), 43 (replication) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Additional Results}
\label{app:additional}

\subsection{Top-$k$ Feature Trajectories}
\label{app:topk_trajectories}

Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory} shows feature trajectories for nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) and temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) sampling.
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) trajectories follow the same monotonically decreasing trend for all features but with compressed inter-depth spacing, consistent with the signal attenuation effect discussed in \S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}.
Concretely, the d0$\to$d1 drop in \texttt{p90\_ppl} under top-$k$ is 78\% (vs.\ 87\% for nucleus), and the d2$\to$d3 reduction is 31\% (vs.\ 39\% for nucleus).
Despite the magnitude reduction, feature rank ordering across depths is preserved, which explains why ordinal classifiers maintain directionality even under top-$k$ conditions.

\subsection{Permutation Importance}
\label{app:permutation}

To verify that the Gini-based importance rankings in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features} are not an artifact of the impurity measure, we compute permutation importance (5-fold CV, 10 repeats) for all 9 atlas cells.
The top-2 features are stable across measures: \texttt{p90\_ppl} ranks in the top 2 in 9/9 cells under both Gini and permutation importance, and \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 (vs.\ 7/9 for Gini, with the additional cell being OLMo top-$k$; the single SHAP exception is GPT-2~XL top-$k$, where \texttt{p75\_ppl} displaces \texttt{var\_surprisal} to rank 3).

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 68.9\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.9\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature) four-class (d1--d3 balanced: 58.9--81.5\%), all exceeding the 25\%/33\% chance baselines.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (75.8--85.9\%), followed by OLMo (74.4--82.3\%) and Pythia (68.9--78.7\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 9.3~pp, comparable to the 7.2~pp mean within-strategy model gap; decoding strategy and model family contribute roughly equally to performance variance.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Four-class RF accuracy (\%) with bootstrap 95\% CI (${\pm}$half-width; $B{=}1{,}000$). RF with 200 trees, evaluated via 5-fold stratified CV (seed=42). Parenthesized: d1--d3 balanced accuracy from the same 4-class RF (chance = 25\% / 33\%). Depth-0 recall exceeds 97\% in all cells. Within-distribution ordinal quality: MAE\,=\,0.23, QWK\,=\,0.89 (QWK$_{\text{d1--d3}}$\,=\,0.74); 98.2\% of errors fall on adjacent depths ($|\Delta| \leq 1$).}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
& Nucleus & Temp.\ $\tau{=}0.9$ & Top-$k$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B  & 78.7{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(71.8) & 76.8{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(69.3) & 68.9{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(58.9) \\
OLMo-1B     & 78.9{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(72.7) & 82.3{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(76.6) & 74.4{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(66.2) \\
GPT-2~XL    & \textbf{84.3}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(79.6) & \textbf{85.9}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.4}\,(81.5) & \textbf{75.8}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(68.1) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-class recall.}
Depth-0 recall exceeds 97\% in all 9 cells.
Among generated depths, d2 consistently yields the lowest recall (44--73\%), followed by d3 (61--80\%) and d1 (72--91\%).
The d2 trough reflects its position at the center of the depth gradient, where features overlap with both d1 and d3, consistent with d2-vs-d3 as the EDD bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).
Full per-class recall appears in Appendix~\ref{app:per_class}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Consistency Across the Grid}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Despite the 17.1~pp accuracy spread across cells, the perplexity tail statistics family (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{p75\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) and surprisal variance (\texttt{var\_surprisal}) dominate depth discrimination in every setting.
Within this family, \texttt{p90\_ppl} ranks top-2 by Gini importance in all 9 cells and \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 Gini; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}), though individual rankings should be interpreted cautiously given high multicollinearity (VIF${>}$10 for 14--16/19 features).\footnote{The group-level dominance of perplexity tail statistics is robust across importance measures (Gini, SHAP, permutation; Kendall's $W{=}0.90$, $p{<}0.005$).}
Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance} shows the full per-cell ranking.

Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$ (range $[0.619, 0.954]$): models agree on which features carry depth information even when they differ in absolute accuracy.
Among strategies, top-$k$ yields the highest cross-model rank correlation ($\rho = 0.811$): the deterministic vocabulary truncation imposes a more uniform feature landscape across architectures, at the cost of weaker overall signal (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).

Feature ablation confirms this concentration: the top-5 features achieve 97.6\% of full accuracy (76.5\% vs.\ 78.4\%), with perplexity statistics alone reaching 76.6\% (vs.\ surprisal 73.2\%, other categories ${\leq}$47\%).

The feature hierarchy shifts with depth.
At shallow boundaries (d1 vs.\ d2), \texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates importance; at deeper boundaries (d3 vs.\ d4, d4 vs.\ d5), tail perplexity statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) take over.
This transition aligns with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursion narrows the surprisal distribution (captured by its variance), while later steps erode the perplexity tail (captured by upper quantiles and kurtosis).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top-5 features by Gini importance. \textbf{Bold}: top-2. \texttt{p90\_ppl} top-2 in 9/9; \texttt{var\_surprisal} 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini).}
\label{tab:feature_importance}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\toprule
Cell & Top-5 features (Gini importance, ranked) \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc.       & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.15)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.08), p75\,(.07) \\
Pythia, $\tau$     & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.14)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), p75\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.07) \\
Pythia, top-$k$    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.12)}, m\_ppl\,(.11), var\_ppl\,(.07), m\_surp\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, nuc.    & \textbf{var\_surp\,(.21)}, \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.06) \\
GPT-2\,XL, $\tau$  & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.25)}, \textbf{p75\,(.15)}, m\_surp\,(.13), var\_surp\,(.12), p50\,(.05) \\
GPT-2\,XL, top-$k$ & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.24)}, \textbf{p75\,(.12)}, var\_surp\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.04) \\
OLMo, nuc.         & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.18)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.15)}, p75\,(.11), m\_surp\,(.07), ttr\,(.06) \\
OLMo, $\tau$       & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.21)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.16)}, p75\,(.12), m\_surp\,(.09), ttr\,(.05) \\
OLMo, top-$k$      & \textbf{p90\_ppl\,(.20)}, \textbf{var\_surp\,(.17)}, p75\,(.10), m\_surp\,(.07), m\_ppl\,(.05) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_atlas_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Atlas feature importance heatmap. Rows: 19 features grouped by category (\S\ref{sec:method:features}); columns: 9 model--strategy cells. Color intensity encodes RF Gini importance. The perplexity-tail and surprisal-variance blocks maintain high importance across all cells.}
\label{fig:atlas_heatmap}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Monotonicity and Inter-Depth Distances}
\label{sec:atlas:monotonicity}

Depth features exhibit strong monotonic trends: perplexity tail statistics decrease with depth (Spearman $\rho = -1.0$ across cell-level medians) while repetition features increase (Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory}).
Inter-depth distances are non-uniform: d0-vs-d1 AUC exceeds 0.99 in all 9 cells, whereas d2-vs-d3 ranges from 0.69 to 0.89.

% Feature trajectory figure moved to Appendix for space

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{EDD Analysis}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

We extend the benchmark to depth $d{=}5$ for all three models under nucleus sampling.
Each model's depth-3 LoRA checkpoint is used to finetune and generate depth-4 data, and the process repeats for depth~5; feature extraction uses the same reference model (Pythia-1.4B).
We train dedicated binary RF classifiers (500 trees) for each adjacent pair, as defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}.

\paragraph{AUC decay and cross-model convergence.}
For Pythia-1.4B (nucleus), AUC decays monotonically from $0.999$ (d0v1) to $0.683$ (d4v5); AUC-excess contraction ratios (0.80 at d1v2$\to$d2v3, 0.69 at d3v4$\to$d4v5) show convergence toward indistinguishability accelerates with depth (Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay}).
The d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters near $T{=}0.75$ for all three models (Pythia $0.784$, OLMo $0.765$, GPT-2~XL $0.750$), while d4-vs-d5 uniformly falls below $0.70$.
Bootstrap testing ($H_0{:}\, \mathrm{AUC} \leq 0.75$) yields $p = 0.001$ (Pythia), $0.091$ (OLMo), $0.505$ (GPT-2~XL).

\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
Across the 9-cell benchmark (depths 0--3), all cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$ at $T{=}0.65$; at $T{=}0.70$, 8/9 cells; at $T{=}0.75$, 7/9; at $T{=}0.80$, 5/9.
Top-$k$ configurations lose $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$ first (Pythia top-$k$ at $T{=}0.70$, OLMo top-$k$ at $T{=}0.75$); d2-vs-d3 is the consistent bottleneck.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Binary pairwise AUC decay curves for three models (nucleus sampling, depths 0--5). Horizontal dashed lines mark $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$. All three curves converge below 0.70 at d4-vs-d5, while d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters in the range 0.750--0.784. Shaded bands: bootstrap 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Trajectories}
\label{sec:atlas:strategy}

The three strategies induce qualitatively distinct degradation trajectories.
Temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) yields the highest accuracy in 2/3 models (GPT-2~XL 85.9\%, OLMo 82.3\%) because uniform logit scaling accelerates convergence to an attractor~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}, amplifying the fingerprint but creating a boundary reversal risk under cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) produces the lowest accuracy (68.9--75.8\%) because hard vocabulary truncation removes depth-discriminative low-probability tokens.
The strategy effect (9.3\,pp range) exceeds the model effect (7.2\,pp); deployment-time knowledge of the decoding strategy matters at least as much as the generator model.

